on't think i was fully thinking you  know the population bomb  the rockets heading off the graph and i  don't think i was  thinking in apocalyptic terms  necessarily but  i do think that i was thinking still  very much in terms of numbers  and so i think probably the biggest  turn that came in my mind and it was  something that i tried to replicate in  the film because i think that's the way  that these kind of films work best is  when you remember what surprised you  what made you  have a different perspective was this  idea that  it's not about adjusting the numbers  and making people conform to the numbers  and that's how you solve people's  problems it's the exact opposite  you broke the movie into three chapters  each in a different part of the world  each kind of exemplifying a different  aspect of this debate  and the first chapter was set in china  where it's  very well known at this point that the  one child policy  has been a giant backfire lots  of social problems there you follow a 29  year old  man who is searching for a wife  in a pool where there aren't as many  single women as there are single men  do you think that governments are going  to keep  trying to do these kinds of things to  control the population  and should they be doing anything along  those lines  i think that there will always be  governments that will  intervene in a way that will prove to  have unintended consequences and that's  what that story is about  um yes you know pop fertility numbers  dropped but  there are a lot of um other issues that  that came up and now  of course china's trying to relax the  policy but you know hans rosaling says  you know fertility rates dropped in  china not just because of the one child  policy  there's other places in asia where  fertility also dropped it's also about  access to family planning  it's about economic opportunity and so  these young women they don't necessarily  want to get married at 22 and go live  with their  husbands family they want to have their  uh education their careers  and maybe they want to get married when  they're 30. but this is the 30 year old  man who  want to marry the 22 year old so it's  like this  love triangle dynamic that's being  played out on a national scale in these  urban centers and there's a lot of  pressure on  on young people and also on the  traditional mindset you're talking about  kind of the individual choices that  people are making that  governments aren't able to predict  necessarily  and you document a lot of top-down  approaches like that whether it's the  chinese policy or  governments giving away cars to have  people sterilize themselves or  give away cars to have people have more  babies what is the role  of personal choice in solving  or kind of abating the overpopulation  right so the example of giving away cars  is i think a perfect example  of a disconnect the people who  participate in  these kind of campaigns you know they  get sterilized they get a chance to win  a little car  they have more babies in russia they  have a chance to win a big car  if you talk to those families as we did  they were going to make those decisions  anyway it didn't have anything to do  with the chance at winning a vehicle  so i think again it's looking at what do  people really need what do they really  want  so when we're looking at individual  choices and what people need  if you look at the example in uganda  it's it's really about the basics it's  about having  access to education health care it's  autonomy  it's a poverty alleviation it's all the  basics  i think the film is about trying to look  at the issue  from a different perspective and to ask  ourselves are we are we  asking the right questions are we still  in this old mindset where we think it's  all about overpopulation  because it's not what was really  striking in the movie  is the way that when women's choices in  particular  are constrained the fertility rate goes  up  what role does expanding the individual  rights of women and protecting women's  rights in particular  play into this debate when women have  access to more choices  it doesn't mean that they stop having  kids and it doesn't mean that  they're necessarily going to have uh  more more kids what we find is that  when women have access to again all the  things that you need to have a decent  chance at life  they tend to gravitate towards two  children per woman  obviously there's some variation but i  think what you see is that in giving  more opportunity  it's a stabilizing influence i think  there's a there also is a is a  misconception about the idea that if  people have  maybe more recesses so more opportunity  that they're going to have more kids  that somehow there's a darwinian thing  going on if you deny things from people  then they'll have fewer kids because  they can't take care of them  that's not how it works the fear that  people have about  overpopulation often is that in these  other countries whether it's india or  china  that they're just going to keep growing  and they're going to consume all the  earth's resources  that doesn't seem to be accurate i think  in wealthier countries  there tends to be a lot of hand-wringing  about  high fertility rates people having too  many babies because somehow we think  that's going to take away from what we  have  but really what we should be concerned  about is that high fertility rates  happen in places where that's an  indication that there  is a lot of suffering on the ground in a  lot of places a lot of countries in  sub-saharan africa which  uh very high poverty levels women don't  have a lot of choices  people tend it's almost like a visceral  darwinian kind of  instinct people have to try to just uh  you know it's it's fearful i think for  them to think about the masses  that fearful kind of visceral reaction  given that it's so ingrained in human  nature really to think about  things as just a shrinking pie  is there any way to overcome that and  how does it  affect how we think about these issues  it's a  great question because i think that that  fear  it doesn't inform the bait it actually  prevents the debate we hide behind fear  it's  such an it's a way to be able to um  avoid dealing with the problem because  if something is scary and it's  overwhelming and there's nothing you can  do  then you can back away so i think uh  taking the cue from hans rosling the  idea is wait a minute let's look at what  we're really dealing with  you thought it was this big scary thing  that's not actually what's happening  there are these pockets where there's  things need to change there's other  things that actually are doing quite  well let's  really take a realistic look so i think  in that way um  there's something that can be very  helpful just about  basic knowledge and i think that was one  thing that was really eye-opening is to  realize  some very basic things about what's  happening  in the world right now that are not um  that are not widely known and it drives  hans crazy  the movie is misconception premiering at  tribeca jessica you thank you very much  for talking to us  it's been a pleasure thank you for  reason tv i'm zach weissmuller
 

